{"author":{"id":"698145999e8f05e141e1b3e41afea48de9004817a883a692985b3265ceb5f427"},"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"698145999e8f05e141e1b3e41afea48de9004817a883a692985b3265ceb5f427"},"timestamp":1781365153,"metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDODbKJfs8AAAABFYUvJg","github-url":"https://github.com/vmiklos/plees-tracker/issues/584","origin":"github"},"nonce":"Xj9mJ+EQWh50PwjX/7qcrqTlTJc=","title":"add new graph for sleep schedule","message":"I have a bad sleeping pattern, my days are not 24 hours long, but about 25-25.5 hours long. This makes me go to bed later-and-later every day, which makes my sleep schedule drifting away slowly from the norm.\n\nI have tried maybe 200 sleep tracking apps in the last 15 years (serious), none of them had any tools for a sleep disorder like this, not even a just read-only visualization. Most of the apps does not even show your sleep schedule in any form, and majority of which do, uses a visualization, that suits people with normal sleeping patterns. So, the sleep tracking apps cater to people who don't have sleeping problems... The closest i've got was the Google Fit app, but that still used a relative time scale and a tiny chart.\n\nI would like to ask to include a chart that looks something like this:\n\n\u003cimg width=\"833\" height=\"1500\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f2ef3a6e-9ec6-4b53-8290-b8895bb0c2e8\" /\u003e\n\nThe main rule is to make the time axis totally fixed to the unit of the day. Having a relative, moving axis can not visualize an irregular sleep schedule.\nI use the yellow background from 6h-21h just to help normal people, like my doctor, understand the chart better. It could be any time interval, i just thought, that is the socially accepted awake time range.\n\nIt would be nice to have the chart in portrait orientation, because in landscape, one day takes up unnecessary too much screen space, and the months get squished/trimmed, you see less days, you get less context to have the great picture visualized.\nAlso, it would be nice to have the chart in up-to-down time orientation, like how Europeans read text: the top was earlier, the bottom is later. The scrolling should start from the bottom to upwards. My experience with doctors is, when the time axis was the other way around (current is on the top), people had a hard time grasping what they look at.\n\nI am NOT good at android coding, let me know, what can i help with to make this work.","files":null}]}